## 18. AI Coding Agent Rules

The AI coding agent must:

- Follow this architecture.
- Keep the project beginner-friendly.
- Avoid unnecessary libraries.
- Avoid over-engineering.
- Work on one feature at a time.
- Explain the approach before implementation.
- Explain important generated code.
- Provide test cases for each feature.
- Respect existing code instead of rewriting unrelated parts.
- Ask for clarification when requirements conflict.
- Never place secrets directly in source code.
- Never assume that code is correct merely because it runs.

The human developer must inspect, run, test, and understand generated code.

---

## 19. Definition of Done

The project is considered complete when:

- All core features work.
- Normal and edge-case inputs have been tested.
- Frontend and backend communicate correctly.
- MongoDB Atlas stores the data.
- `.env` files and secrets are excluded from GitHub.
- The project is pushed to GitHub.
- Backend is deployed on Render.
- Frontend is deployed on Render.
- Deployed frontend can communicate with deployed backend.
- The developer can explain the project structure and code.

---

## 20. Important Constraint

Do NOT ask the AI coding agent to generate the complete application in one shot.

Use the workflow:

```text
Understand
   ↓
Plan
   ↓
Prompt
   ↓
Generate
   ↓
Inspect
   ↓
Run
   ↓
Test
   ↓
Debug
   ↓
